Subjective information like patient's physical and psychological state, patient motivation, patient feedback, and therapists' own work and life experience were among the factors that played crucial role, as well as prioritized, in clinical reasoning of physiotherapists besides just objective data (both motor and physiological). This clinical reasoning helped them set patient goals, decide of treatment plan and adjust rehabilitation sessions (Molle et al., 2025; Holdar et al., 2013). Additionally, highlighting the need to incorporate patient feedbacks in rehabilitation technology. It suggest therapists should be in the loop instead of adopting full automation (Molle et al., 2025). Novice physical therapists rely more on informative factors for clinical decision making, while experienced physical therapists rely more on directive factors for decision making(Holdar et al., 2013).

== Therapist Interfaces for Exergame-Based Therapy
Various therapist interfaces for personalized rehabilitation through Exergames have been developed, although they do not account for the entirety of the therapy process as they mainly focus on data documentation and/or session planning and task manipulation. One therapist interface for Village Druid VR, a therapy game used for rehabilitation was prototyped along through 2 co-design sessions with 2 therapists each. The lo-fi and hi-fi prototypes focused on patient data documentation for monitoring and progress evaluation as well as some simpler personalization session parameters which were not clearly defined (Ferreira et al., 2022). Similarly, Duval et al. (2022) redesigned an already existing conventional VR game, Spellcasters, through co-design sessions with 14 medical professionals (PT's & OT's). The re-purposed version of Spellcasters focused again solely on data documentation deemed valuable by the medical professionals, and task management through custom rehabilitative gestures to adjust relevant variables including scale, shape, direction and depth.

Another more elaborative study developed a set of collaborative VR serious games, 4 in total, both for upper-limb physical rehabilitation and cognitive rehabilitation of stroke survivors. Again, their framework ADAPT (Adaptive Dynamics for Activity-based Personalized Therapy) only focused on personalized session planning and some game task parameters. For the session planning, therapists could adjust the number of participants, the selection and sequencing of games, session durations and individualized goals. For game task parameters, the therapist could adjust level of difficulty and more detailed adjustments in complexity such as the number and type of objects or puzzle pieces, and in-game payment processes (Figueiredo et al., 2025; ). 

One study in particular fully isolated game task adjustment in the form of difficulty parameters only. Difficulty in classification in levels were found to lead to longer playtime with more fun and higher performance when compared to high-commitment game parameter goals in terms of goal and time (Eckert et al., 2023). Gagnon-Roy et al. (2024) on the other hand co-developed COOK, a cognitive assistance system together with occupational therapists to help patients regain the ability to prepare meals. Although not an exergame, the patient still has a personalized interface with step-by-step instructions to follow where breaks, reminders and recipe-steps are all pre-configured by the therapist. 

Indisputably, with one exception, previous research does not account for how to share the agency between the therapist and the system. Figueiredo et al. (2025), the exception, highlight in their study that 7 out of 10 participants preferred a hybrid mode combining both automated and manual adjustments, however focused on task personalization only as well. Furthermore, while data documentation does improve evaluation and decision-making and personalized therapy and decision-making, ultimately improving patient care (Tadas et al., 2023;Dawdling et al., 2015 ), the current systems still remain very fragmented as they lack a holistic view of all the main phases in the therapeutic process. The Ocuppational Intervention Process model includes (1) Evaluation and Goal-Setting Phase, Intervention Phase, (3) Reevaluation Phase (Hinojosa et al., 2017). Physiotherapy largely uses the same basic structure and is often referred to as the "rehab cycle" or "SOAP" which also includes (1) Assessment and goal setting, (2) intervention and (3) re-evaluation (Negrini, 2019; Physiopedia, n.d.; Physiofirstt; 2023). 

== More-than-Human Design

Recent HCI discussions of post-interaction or "fourth wave" present that designers must understand the shifting paradigm of human-computer relations including how agency and responsibility are distributed between people and computational systems (Mensah, 2025). More-than-human design came about as a response to the limitaiton of human-centered design in addressing complex socio-technical and ecological challenges (Giacardi & Redström, 2020).  The idea of human-centered design must be revisited to account for the world where it is no longer exclusively humans that act, design, make use, and change. 

More-than-human design offers a theoretical lens for understanding rehabiliation exergames as an integrated collection of humans (therapists and patients) and non-humans (such as exergame software, algorithms, data representations, sensors, infrastructure), the agency of which are continuously negotiated (Giacardi & Redström, 2020). As these system become more data-driven, therapy decisions are increasingly influenced by algorithmic logic and historical data apart from just therapist reasoning () This reiterates the fact that design in therapeutic scenarios are less about optimizing interfaces of users and more about configuring relationships and agencies among multiple human and nonhuman actors over time. 

