To provide precision rehabilitation, technology development can be approached through rule-based system, biofeedback, intelligent algorithms, contextual adaptation and behavioral tracking, with future directions focused on large-scale diverse datasets and therapists oversight (Figueiredo, 2025). However, these studies are still approached from anthropocentric perspective and fail to consider the unfolding future in which the networked computational components are informed by algorithmic logic without human oversight. We need to ultimately be open to conversation about possible future instead of a single inevitable future. 

In HCI, agency is the sense of the subjective experience of being in control of actions and their effects in the world and not only a matter of who executes a task.This sense of agency can be affected by factors such as timing, predictability, and level or form of automation (Legaspi et al, 2024; Limerick, 2014). Experimental studies indicate that higher level of automation and increased mental workload are associated with reduced sense of agency, whereas carefully designed hybrid interactions can maintain a stronger feeling of control even when systems act on user's behalf (Howard et. al, 2016; Legaspi et al, 2024). These findings are particularly relevant in clinical contexts where therapists must be able to stand by decisions, both ethically and legally, even when those clinical decisions are mediated by automated systems. 
